{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"B\n    \n      Relational / intent tree\n      LLM declares relationships, never positions: \"R7,R8 form a column from VCC to GND\", \"C3 hangs from the OUT node\", \"decoupling group sits left of U1, below the +5V rail\". Engine resolves a relative-placement graph to coordinates. Captures why, robust to spacing — but a richer schema the LLM must learn.\n      divider-filter:\nrails: {top: VCC, bottom: GND}\ncolumn divider [R7, R8]:\n  top->VCC  bottom->GND\n  midtap -> port OUT (right)\nshunt C3:\n  from: divider.midtap  to: GND\nports: {OUT: right}\n# engine owns ALL placement & routing\n# from these relations","choice":"relational","id":null,"timestamp":1781368930883}
{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"B\n    \n      Relational / intent tree\n      LLM declares relationships, never positions: \"R7,R8 form a column from VCC to GND\", \"C3 hangs from the OUT node\", \"decoupling group sits left of U1, below the +5V rail\". Engine resolves a relative-placement graph to coordinates. Captures why, robust to spacing — but a richer schema the LLM must learn.\n      divider-filter:\nrails: {top: VCC, bottom: GND}\ncolumn divider [R7, R8]:\n  top->VCC  bottom->GND\n  midtap -> port OUT (right)\nshunt C3:\n  from: divider.midtap  to: GND\nports: {OUT: right}\n# engine owns ALL placement & routing\n# from these relations","choice":"relational","id":null,"timestamp":1781368931322}
{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"A\n    \n      Coarse slot-grid + annotations\n      LLM places each part on a small unitless integer grid (col,row) + orientation, and tags rails/ports. Engine expands cells to real spacing, builds rails, routes. Like an ASCII sketch — LLMs are good at this; no real geometry, just ranks & cells.\n      divider-filter:\nrail  +VCC  band=top\nrail  GND   band=bottom\nplace R7  col=1 row=1  vert\nplace R8  col=1 row=3  vert\nplace C3  col=2 row=3  vert\nnode  at col1.row2 -> port OUT side=right\n# engine: col->x, row->y, snap, rails, route\n\n   the LLM literally \"draws\" a coarse\n   2-col x 3-row grid; engine scales it","choice":"grid","id":null,"timestamp":1781368932285}
{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"C\n    \n      Hybrid: coarse grid for the skeleton, relations for the rest (my recommendation)\n      LLM puts the anchors (ICs) and groups on a coarse grid for global flow (the part it must get right), and uses a few relational tags for passive attachment (\"C1,C2 decouple +5V→GND\", \"OUT exits right\"). Best of both: robust global flow + intent for the fiddly local wiring. Engine still owns every coordinate.\n      mcp1703:\nflow: left-to-right\nrails: {+5V top, +3V3 top, GND bottom}\ngrid:  F1@c0  U1@c1  [D1,R2]@c2\ngroup input_decoup [C1,C2]:\n  decouple +5V -> GND   under: +5V@c0..c1\ngroup out_decoup  [C3,C4]:\n  decouple +3V3 -> GND  under: +3V3@c1..c2\nports: {5V_BUS: left}","choice":"hybrid","id":null,"timestamp":1781368933747}
{"source":"user-event","type":"click","text":"A\n    \n      Coarse slot-grid + annotations\n      LLM places each part on a small unitless integer grid (col,row) + orientation, and tags rails/ports. Engine expands cells to real spacing, builds rails, routes. Like an ASCII sketch — LLMs are good at this; no real geometry, just ranks & cells.\n      divider-filter:\nrail  +VCC  band=top\nrail  GND   band=bottom\nplace R7  col=1 row=1  vert\nplace R8  col=1 row=3  vert\nplace C3  col=2 row=3  vert\nnode  at col1.row2 -> port OUT side=right\n# engine: col->x, row->y, snap, rails, route\n\n   the LLM literally \"draws\" a coarse\n   2-col x 3-row grid; engine scales it","choice":"grid","id":null,"timestamp":1781368935540}
